# TagKit

> Copy-paste page templates and snippets in plain semantic HTML. TagBrush.css styles them, TagAction makes them behave, and every one passes TagCheck (no errors or warnings, WCAG AA contrast in light and dark mode, no sideways scrolling on phones). Start a page from a template, or paste a snippet into a page, then replace the example content.

## How to use it

- Fetch the template or snippet from its URL below and copy it as it is. Change the words, links, numbers, and images; keep the elements and attributes.
- A template is a complete page with the TagBrush.css stylesheet and the TagAction script in `<head>`.
- A snippet goes inside `<main>` of a page that already has them, unless it is marked page-level, in which case it replaces or sits beside the page's own `<header>`, `<main>`, `<aside>`, or `<footer>` directly in `<body>`.
- Ids in a snippet (`orders-table`, `delete-project`, `account-menu`) must stay unique: rename them, and every attribute that points at them (`aria-controls`, `commandfor`), when you use a snippet twice.
- Don't add classes, wrapper `<div>`s, or CSS for layout. If something looks wrong, check the HTML against the TagBrush.css guide instead.
- Run `npx @kovirlabs/tagcheck page.html` when you are done.

## Build-time includes

Share a header, nav, and footer between static pages without a framework. In each page, put an include comment where the piece goes, and keep the pieces in a folder whose name starts with `_`:

```html
<body>
<!--#include file="_includes/header.html" -->
<main>…</main>
<!--#include virtual="/_includes/footer.html" -->
</body>
```

Then build: `npx @kovirlabs/tagkit build site/ public/` (`--check` also runs TagCheck). `file="…"` is relative to the page and `virtual="/…"` to the site folder. Pages get their includes filled in; other files are copied; names starting with `_` are left out. Links to the page being built get `aria-current="page"`, and root links (`/about.html`) in included pieces become relative, so write links in shared pieces from the site root. Don't use JavaScript or iframes to include headers and footers.

## Themes

- [Pandoc template](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kovirlabs/tagkit/main/themes/pandoc/template.html): turn Markdown, Word, or any Pandoc input into a styled page: `pandoc notes.md --standalone --toc --template https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kovirlabs/tagkit/main/themes/pandoc/template.html -o notes.html`. Front matter `primary-color` sets the brand color; `no-tagaction: true` leaves out the script. Add `--mathjax` for equations.
- [Jekyll theme](https://github.com/kovirlabs/tagkit-jekyll): a GitHub Pages site styled with one config line, `remote_theme: kovirlabs/tagkit-jekyll` (and `plugins: [jekyll-remote-theme]`). Layouts `home`, `page`, `post`; `toc: true` adds a table of contents. Guide: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kovirlabs/tagkit-jekyll/main/llms.txt
- [MkDocs theme](https://github.com/kovirlabs/tagkit/tree/main/themes/mkdocs): `pip install "mkdocs-tagkit @ git+https://github.com/kovirlabs/tagkit#subdirectory=themes/mkdocs"`, then `theme: { name: tagkit }` in mkdocs.yml (options `primary_color`, `tagaction: false`). Site nav as a sidebar, a table of contents beside each page, previous/next links. No search yet.
- [Hugo theme](https://github.com/kovirlabs/tagkit/tree/main/themes/hugo): import the module `github.com/kovirlabs/tagkit/themes/hugo`, or `npx degit kovirlabs/tagkit/themes/hugo themes/tagkit` and `theme = "tagkit"`. A blog with a home page of post cards, `toc: true` for a table of contents, and `[params.tagkit]` for `primaryColor`, `dateFormat`, `tagaction`.

